  "sourceUnverified": "source not yet verified",
  "src_A1A2": "2.5 % of the zakatable base once per lunar year: Abu Dawud 1573 (A1). A solar year at 2.577 %: AAOIFI Shari'ah Standard 35, reported by the National Zakat Foundation UK (A2) — section number not yet verified. Other scholars (Hanafi, Mufti Online) keep exactly 2.5 % and the lunar date.",
  "src_B1": "Cash and bank balances are zakatable in full: Quran 9:34–35; Abu Dawud 1573; IIFA Res 21 (9/3) (B1).",
  "src_B2": "Gold and silver held as savings: zakat on market value (Quran 9:34; Abu Dawud 1573). Worn jewellery: Hanafis say zakat is due (Abu Dawud 1563); Malikis, Shafi'is and Hanbalis say it is not (B2).",
  "src_B3": "Trading shares: 100 % of market value (IIFA Res 28 (3/4) §3, Res 121 (3/13)). Held long term with no company data: dividends only (IIFA Res 28 §3); or 25 % of value as a proxy (National Zakat Foundation UK); or full value as a precaution. A company that pays zakat itself: shareholder does not pay again (IIFA Res 28 §1) (B3).",
  "src_B4": "Funds and ETFs follow the share rules at fund level. Sukuk: 100 % of face or market value by default, with override (B4); AAOIFI SS 35 and SS 17 section numbers not yet verified.",
  "src_B5": "Personal-use property is not zakatable. Rental property: zakat is not on the property; rent still held counts with cash (IIFA Res 2 (2/2)). Bought to resell: 100 % of market value (B5).",
  "src_B8": "Scholars differ on whether holding crypto is permitted (IIFA Res 237 (8/24) gave no final ruling). Those who permit it say zakat is due on its full market value (B8).",
  "src_Z6": "Interest is not the owner's wealth: it is not return and not in the zakat base, and it is given away (Quran 2:278–279). Giving it away does not count as zakat (B1). AAOIFI SS 21 section not yet verified.",
  "src_A6": "Zakat is always on the actual value on the zakat date, never on an inflation-adjusted value (IIFA Res 28 (3/4) §3). The real return is shown for planning only (A6).",
  "src_A3": "Nisab: gold 85 g (20 dinars) and silver 595 g (200 dirhams): Abu Dawud 1573; Bukhari 1447; Muslim 979. Which metal to use for cash is a point of difference (A3).",
