  "zakatMoveUmmAlQura": "Umm al-Qura",
  "zakatMoveSolarLine": "You can move a solar date earlier only. Giving early is allowed; giving later delays zakat.",
  "zakatMoveInfo": "All figures and the notification use the new date. Next year is not affected.",
  "src_A4": "One zakat date a year. If your wealth held nisab for a full lunar year, zakat is due on all zakatable wealth you hold that day, including money added during the year: Abu Dawud 1573; IIFA Res 28 (3/4) §4. Shafi'i and Hanbali schools give new wealth its own year from the day it was acquired; this app uses the single date. Nisab is checked on the due date only. Hanafi: nisab must exist at the start and the end of the year (A4).",
  "src_A5": "Debts you must pay within 12 months may be deducted from zakatable wealth. Scholars differ on long-term debts such as a mortgage: deduct the next 12 months of instalments, deduct all, or deduct none. The Shafi'i view is that debt does not reduce zakat (al-Nawawi, al-Majmu') — source not yet verified. AAOIFI SS 35 lists deductible liabilities — section numbers not yet verified (A5, C4).",
  "src_A7": "Every currency is money for zakat (IIFA Res 21 (9/3)). Convert each holding at the rate on the due date (Darul Iftaa Mahmudiyyah; no AAOIFI or IIFA text found on the rate date) and add them (National Zakat Foundation UK). Giving zakat in another currency is allowed (A7).",
  "src_A8": "Gold, silver and all other wealth are added together by value. Hanafis and Malikis combine by value; Shafi'is do not combine gold and silver — primary source not yet verified (A8).",
  "src_B9": "A good debt (the debtor is able and willing to pay) is zakatable every year, as if it were cash in hand. A doubtful debt has no zakat until it is received (IIFA Res 1 (1/2); Saudi Permanent Committee) (B9).",
  "src_Z4": "Based on the lines of this year only. Extra lines take cash and good debts only (IIFA Res 1 (1/2)); gold, goods for sale and a business belong in My Investments. Nisab is priced as grams of the metal you chose times its rate on the due date; the pricing rule is not yet verified (Z-4, C5).",
  "src_C1": "Zakat falls due when 12 lunar months have passed since your wealth first reached nisab: Abu Dawud 1573; Quran 9:36 and 2:189; Darul Iftaa Birmingham. If the date is not known, use a best estimate — common fatwa practice, source not yet verified (C1).",
  "src_C2": "Zakat that is already due must not be pushed later. Most scholars hold that zakat is given when it falls due and not delayed without a valid reason (Saudi Permanent Committee, islamqa 13981; primary texts not yet verified). Hanafi: a zakat date may not be changed; you may give early on an estimate, then check the amount on the real date and make up any shortfall (FatwaCentre, 178985) (C2).",
  "src_C3": "A lunar month starts when the new moon is seen and has 29 or 30 days (Bukhari 1909 and 1907; Quran 2:189). Umm al-Qura is a calculated calendar and can differ from local sighting by a day or two. Using it is a product choice, and moving the date by up to 2 days is the consultant's reading — source not yet verified. A cycle day 30 in a 29-day month uses the 29th — reading not yet verified. A solar date may move earlier only (C3).",
  "src_C4": "Value all zakatable wealth on the due date and take off the debts you deduct. If what is left reaches nisab, zakat is due (Hanafi: Ibn Abidin, Radd al-Muhtar 2/260, via Council of Ulama Eastern Cape). Shafi'i: debt does not cancel zakat; in the app that is deduction off. Below nisab, nothing is due (Abu Dawud 1573) (C4).",
  "src_C5": "A good debt counts in full every year; a doubtful debt counts nothing until it is received (IIFA Res 1 (1/2)). Other wealth (gold, goods for sale, a business) goes in My Investments (C5).",
  "src_C6": "Giving zakat before it falls due is allowed: Abu Dawud 1624 (Ali; al-Abbas asked to give early and the Prophet allowed it; graded hasan by al-Albani, da'if by Zubair Ali Zai). Conditions: you hold nisab when you give it and you give it as zakat; the Shafi'i school limits it to one year ahead (Darul Iftaa Jordan, Q 2974); Hanafi: give early, then check the amount on the real date (FatwaCentre 178985). The Maliki school accepts this only shortly before the due date — source not yet verified (C6).",
  "src_C7": "Hanafi: an extra amount given as zakat may count toward next year (Darul Uloom Deoband, fatwa 28/76/D=01/1435). Shafi'i: the extra is sadaqah, because each year's zakat needs its own intention (Darul Iftaa Jordan, Q 3602). The app does not move it, which is valid under both views (C7).",
  "src_C8": "Scholars differ on the exchange rate that counts zakat given in another currency. The rate on the day you gave: Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (the Sahibayn); several contemporary Hanafi muftis prefer it (Muftionline 46417; fatwa-ca 178598). The rate on the due date: Abu Hanifa (Darul Iftaa Mahmudiyyah 134900, which allows a higher rate). The primary text, Radd al-Muhtar, is not yet verified (C8).",
  "src_C9": "Zakat due is the correct amount on the due date, not the first figure the app worked out. What you already gave counts toward the corrected figure, and any shortfall is still owed (FatwaCentre 178985; SeekersGuidance, Mawlana Ilyas Patel). If the corrected figure is lower than what you gave, see C7 (C9).",
  "src_C10": "Nothing is due below 20 dinars or 200 dirhams (Abu Dawud 1573). The notification says only that your zakat year ends, because the app has not yet checked that your wealth reaches nisab (C10).",
  "src_C11": "Zakat is correct only when it is given as zakat to the eight groups named in Quran 9:60, and each year's zakat needs its own intention (Darul Iftaa Jordan, Q 3602). Sadaqah given in good faith to someone who did not qualify still counted as sadaqah (Bukhari 1421; Muslim 1022). Whether that also covers obligatory zakat is disputed (Abu Hanifa and Muhammad: yes, if the giver took care; Abu Yusuf: no) — not yet verified, and the app does not judge it. Interest given away is not zakat (Z-6) (C11).",
